  "slug": "contains-duplicate-ii",
  "title": "Contains Duplicate II",
  "difficulty": "Easy",
  "category": "Sliding window",
  "patterns": [
    "sliding window",
    "hash set"
  ],
  "lists": [
    "interview-150"
  ],
  "hints": [
    "you only ever need to compare each element to the k elements before it, not to every other element in the array. what does that suggest about the window you need to maintain?",
    "what if you kept a set of exactly the last k values seen, and checked each new value against it?",
    "for each element, first check if it's already in the set (a match within range), then add it, then remove the element that just fell outside the window (more than k positions back)",
    "the set never needs to hold more than k elements at once"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "checking against every previous element instead of only the last k, which degrades to O(n^2)",
    "removing the wrong element from the window, or removing before checking instead of after",
    "off-by-one on what 'at most k apart' means (k itself is a valid gap, not just gaps smaller than k)"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given a list of integers, `nums`, and a number `k`.\n\n**Determine whether there are two equal values in `nums` whose indices are at most `k` apart.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= nums.length <= 10^5",
      "-10^9 <= nums[i] <= 10^9",
      "0 <= k <= 10^6"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "k = 0 (indices must be the same, so this is always false unless there are truly duplicate positions, which can't happen)",
      "no duplicates at all",
      "k larger than the array length"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
